s` lifecycle (with deliberate non-edges justified: no `deleted` state, no demotion, no `refuted` re-admission), and a validation pipeline showing which layer owns which invariant.\n\n## The load-bearing decision, and a caveat\n\n§4.2 writes `confidence: float` and then requires it be \"derived, never authored.\" **No schema can tell an authored `0.93` from a derived one** — the documents are byte-identical. Since F-ENVELOPE's acceptance demands an invalid fixture for exactly that, the criteria are only satisfiable if confidence carries its derivation. So I froze it as `{value, method, inputs, computed_at}`, with no `authored`/`frequency` member in the closed `method` enum. The float survives at `confidence.value`, so §5.1's `min_confidence` filter is unchanged.\n\n**This is a deviation from a ratified spec** (SAEOS-1, Founder Proxy 2026-08-06). It is not applied silently — it is routed as clarification **C-1** in §10 and flagged as the first thing a reviewer should challenge.\n\n§6 is candid about limits: the schema makes authored confidence hard to *express*, not impossible to *fake*. Three invariants are only partly reachable statically; each remainder is assigned to a named owner (I-STORE, I-INGEST, slice S3) rather than left implied.\n\n## Verification done at design time\n\nI assembled the schema in memory and ran it against `jsonschema.Draft202012Validator`: `check_schema` passes, and all 15 invalid cases are rejected at the pointer and keyword the design claims, with the valid cases passing clean.\n\nThat run **caught a defect in my own spec**: fixture I03 carried two faults (frequency `method` *and* frequency `input.kind`), violating the single-fault rule I'd stated one section earlier. Split into I03 and I15; corpus is now 14 valid / 15 invalid against minima of 12 / 8. Re-verified — all 15 are rejected, for the declared reason, and single-fault (repair patch yields a valid document).\n\nTwo things I did not do, per scope: no files under `sakg/` or `tests/`